From 2406735b8ecbbc6603c374fd3313120474836b7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: woodenwillis <william.b@millpex.org>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 11:51:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] debug: spin in the aarch64 idle thread instead of wfi

Temporary experiment for the tensor-g4 bring-up; not for upstream.

On caiman the core running a guest vCPU sometimes stops for good at a
point where it waits for outstanding work: a dsb ish after a broadcast
tlbi in the guest kernel, or entry to the EL2 IRQ vector. Meanwhile
the idle cores sit in the idle thread's wfi. If wfi lets an idle core
drop into a low-power state, left enabled by firmware, in which it
doesn't acknowledge broadcast maintenance, the broadcasting core waits
forever.

In debug builds the idle thread now spins on yield, so idle cores
never execute wfi. If the hangs stop, wfi is implicated. Interrupts
stay unmasked in the idle thread, so wakeups are unchanged; idle
cores just burn power.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01CuTDwuvAxZ2dh5tWxWuRxe
---
 src/arch/arm/64/idle.S | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/arch/arm/64/idle.S b/src/arch/arm/64/idle.S
index c47710bcb..db45255fe 100644
--- a/src/arch/arm/64/idle.S
+++ b/src/arch/arm/64/idle.S
@@ -4,11 +4,16 @@
  * SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
  */
 
+#include <config.h>
 #include <machine/assembler.h>
 
 .section .text, "ax"
 
 BEGIN_FUNC(idle_thread)
+#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_BUILD
+    1: yield
+#else
     1: wfi
+#endif
     b 1b
 END_FUNC(idle_thread)
